# Forbidden patterns for Go files (*.go). Applied by .githooks/pre-commit.
# scaffold-extensions: go
# Format: <regex><TAB><description>   (one per line; # comments ignored)
# Separator is TAB - patterns can contain literal `|` for ERE alternation.
# Case-sensitive ERE (grep -anE). Copy to your project as `.forbidden-patterns/go.txt`.
# Active rules flag leftover debug output plus two security sinks (TLS bypass,
# a shell as argv[0]); panic/log.Fatal live in optional below.

(^|[^A-Za-z_.])fmt\.Print(ln|f)?[[:space:]]*\(	Remove fmt.Print/Println/Printf debug output - use the project logger
(^|[^A-Za-z_.])println[[:space:]]*\(	Remove the builtin println() before committing
(^|[^A-Za-z_.])spew\.Dump[[:space:]]*\(	Remove spew.Dump() debug dump before committing

# --- Security: TLS bypass / command injection ---
# `go vet` catches neither and the golangci-lint CI job ships commented out, so
# nothing else in the scaffold sees these. The TLS rule is the Go twin of
# backend.txt's `verify=False` and frontend.txt's `rejectUnauthorized: false`.
# The exec rule fires ONLY when a shell name is the FIRST argument, i.e. argv[0]:
# exec.Command("sh"|"bash"|"zsh"|"ksh"|"dash", ...), optionally /bin/- or
# /usr/bin/-prefixed, and the exec.CommandContext(ctx, "sh", ...) form. A shell
# named in any LATER argument - exec.Command("which", "bash"),
# exec.Command("docker", "run", "alpine", "sh"), exec.Command("apt-get",
# "install", "dash") - and a variable program path - exec.Command(binPath, "-c",
# script) - do NOT fire. What still fires is a constant script,
# exec.Command("bash", "-c", "set -e; make"): that shape is one interpolation
# away from injection, so it is flagged on purpose - scaffold-allow the line, or
# call the program directly.
InsecureSkipVerify[[:space:]]*:[[:space:]]*true	InsecureSkipVerify: true disables TLS cert validation (MITM risk) - fix the cert/CA, don't disable verification
(^|[^A-Za-z_.])exec\.(Command\([[:space:]]*|CommandContext\([^,]*,[[:space:]]*)"(/(usr/)?bin/)?(sh|bash|zsh|ksh|dash)"	Running a shell as argv[0] is a command-injection sink - pass the program and its args to exec.Command directly

# Optional (opt-in) — uncomment a line to enable it:
# (^|[^A-Za-z_.])print[[:space:]]*\(	Remove the builtin print() before committing (off by default: 'print' is a plausible method/field name)
# (^|[^A-Za-z_])panic[[:space:]]*\(	Avoid panic() in library code - return an error (off by default: legitimate in main/init and tests)
# (^|[^A-Za-z_])log\.(Fatal|Panic)(f|ln)?[[:space:]]*\(	Avoid log.Fatal/log.Panic in library code - return an error (off by default: legitimate in main)
# An error dropped with `_` is invisible to every gate the scaffold installs, but
# `_ = f.Close()` in a defer and `x, _ := strconv.Atoi(s)` are idiomatic Go, so an
# always-on rule fires on ordinary correct code. Enable it only where no
# errcheck/golangci-lint runs and every discard should be reviewed.
# (^|[[:space:]])_,?[[:space:]]*(_[[:space:]]*)?:?=[[:space:]]*[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_.]*\(	Error discarded with `_` - handle or wrap it (off by default: also fires on the idiomatic defer `_ = f.Close()`)
